 ## Counterexample
 
 **Given:** Choose a nonzero constant skew-symmetric matrix $\Omega\in\mathbb R^{3\times3}$, with units s$^{-1}$, and the smooth rigid-rotation field $u(t,x)=\Omega x$. Let $\mu>0$ be constant.
 
-1.1 By the velocity-gradient convention [F1], in the original observer $L=\nabla u=\Omega$, so the candidate response is $\tau=\mu\Omega\ne0$. Define $Q(t)=\exp(-\Omega t)$. The matrix-exponential power series and $\Omega^T=-\Omega$ give $Q'=-\Omega Q=-Q\Omega$, $Q^T=Q^{-1}$, and $Q(0)=I_3$. The matrix $Q(t)$ is continuous and orthogonal, so $\det Q(t)\in\{-1,1\}$ is continuous; since $I=\mathbb R$ is connected and $\det Q(0)=1$, $\det Q(t)=1$ for all $t$. Thus $Q(t)\in\mathrm{SO}(3)$. [F1, given, algebra]
+1.1 Work on $I=\mathbb R$ and $U=\mathbb R^3$. By [F3], define $R(t)=\exp(t\Omega)$ and $Q(t)=R(-t)=\exp(-t\Omega)$. They are $C^\infty$, $Q'= -\Omega Q=-Q\Omega$, and $RQ=QR=I_3$. Transposing the operator-norm convergent series term by term, using the bound $\|N^T\|_{\rm op}\le3\|N\|_{\rm op}$ (each entry satisfies $|N_{ij}|\le\|N\|_{\rm op}$ and [F3] bounds the transposed matrix by its Frobenius norm), and $\Omega^T=-\Omega$, gives $R^T=Q$. Thus $R$ and $Q$ are orthogonal. Their determinants are continuous, belong to $\{-1,1\}$, and equal $1$ at $0$; connectedness of $\mathbb R$ makes them $1$ everywhere. Define $\chi(t,a)=R(t)a$. This is $C^2$, each slice is a bijection of $U$ onto $\mathbb R^3$ with $C^2$ inverse $x\mapsto Q(t)x$, $J=\det R=1>0$, and $\chi(0,a)=a$. Moreover $\partial_t\chi=\Omega R(t)a=\Omega\chi(t,a)=u(t,\chi(t,a))$. Hence this motion induces the stated rigid-rotation field and satisfies every motion hypothesis of [F2]. By [F1], $L=\Omega$, so $\tau=\mu\Omega\ne0$. [F1, F3, given, algebra]
 
